    "created_at": "2023-04-14T20:19:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-04-14T20:21:03Z",
    "body": "Thanks @zcorpan!\r\n\r\n> Or is block-size: 100% needed in some cases?\r\n\r\nIt is still needed, yeah.  That's what explains why the orange rect would be centered in cases like dgrogan's initial testcase but with `height` instead of `min-height`, e.g. like this:\r\nhttps://jsfiddle.net/sptfgav9/\r\n\r\n(Without anything like `block-size:100%` on the anonymous box, there's no reason for the anonymous box to be 200px tall in that testcase, and it would just shrinkwrap its orange child's height, instead of growing to fit the fieldset and having room to center its child as it actually interoperably does.)\r\n\r\nI *think* it would be accurate if we **added** your suggested additional text, though; and we should also add the equivalent text for `max-block-size`, to explain why browsers properly center (overflowing off the top & bottom) in this case where the fieldset's only block-size property is a `max-block-size`: https://jsfiddle.net/juLbh9mc/\r\n\r\nA few additional nits on the text:\r\n* You might want to refer to \"used value\" rather than \"computed value\" to avoid implying that there's anything magical that has to happen during the cascade (since computed values are the value-that-inherits, generally determined without layout information).\r\n* The subtractions that you noted (for legend/border/padding) should also be specified for the already-existing `block-size:100%` text as well.  Without those, the spec's `block-size:100%` requirement isn't quite correct.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/1509205930/reactions",
